WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.041 --> 00:00:01.543
It was arguably the most

00:00:01.543 --> 00:00:02.919
unforgettable moment

00:00:02.919 --> 00:00:03.837
in the billion dollar

00:00:03.837 --> 00:00:05.714
blockbuster, Barbie.

00:00:05.714 --> 00:00:08.550
It is literally impossible to be a woman.

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:12.387
We have to always be extraordinary,

00:00:12.470 --> 00:00:16.433
but somehow we're always doing it wrong.

00:00:16.516 --> 00:00:18.560
You have to never get old,

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:20.061
never be rude,

00:00:20.061 --> 00:00:22.897
never show off, never be selfish,

00:00:22.897 --> 00:00:23.857
never fall down,

00:00:23.857 --> 00:00:25.817
never fail, never show fear,

00:00:25.817 --> 00:00:27.902
never get out of line.

00:00:27.902 --> 00:00:31.448
I'm just so tired of watching myself

00:00:31.448 --> 00:00:35.201
and every single other woman

00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:37.078
tie herself into knots

00:00:37.078 --> 00:00:40.081
so that people will like us.

00:00:40.123 --> 00:00:42.375
Yes, the perpetual double standards.

00:00:42.375 --> 00:00:44.753
America Ferrera there with a raw, honest

00:00:44.753 --> 00:00:46.629
and inspirational assessment

00:00:46.629 --> 00:00:48.173
of the impossible standards.

00:00:48.173 --> 00:00:49.632
Women are held to.

00:00:49.632 --> 00:00:50.675
With awards season

00:00:50.675 --> 00:00:52.052
shifting into high gear

00:00:52.052 --> 00:00:53.428
after the Golden Globes,

00:00:53.428 --> 00:00:55.472
where Barbie won for cinematic

00:00:55.472 --> 00:00:57.098
and box office achievement.

00:00:57.098 --> 00:00:59.934
I sat down with Ferrera here in L.A.

00:00:59.934 --> 00:01:01.478
to talk about this Barbie's

00:01:01.478 --> 00:01:03.188
subversive feminism

00:01:03.188 --> 00:01:05.899
and Oscar controversy.

00:01:05.899 --> 00:01:08.026
America Ferrera, welcome to the program.

00:01:08.026 --> 00:01:08.985
Thank you.

00:01:08.985 --> 00:01:12.572
How surprised were you at the huge impact

00:01:12.572 --> 00:01:13.990
that monologue had

00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:15.492
Your speech in Barbie,

00:01:15.492 --> 00:01:18.495
which was already such a successful film?

00:01:18.495 --> 00:01:21.790
It was amazing to see how it hit

00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:22.916
the audiences

00:01:22.916 --> 00:01:24.918
and what the responses were.

00:01:24.918 --> 00:01:26.419
I know that when I

00:01:26.419 --> 00:01:28.755
when I first read the script,

00:01:28.755 --> 00:01:30.256
everything before and after

00:01:30.256 --> 00:01:31.966
and including the monologue,

00:01:31.966 --> 00:01:35.804
we have to always be extraordinary.

00:01:35.887 --> 00:01:39.557
But somehow we're always doing it wrong.

00:01:39.641 --> 00:01:42.811
I know that I was just blown away

00:01:42.894 --> 00:01:46.272
and it was all just so unexpected and.

00:01:46.356 --> 00:01:49.776
And as a woman, I was just so excited,

00:01:49.859 --> 00:01:51.069
you know, that.

00:01:51.069 --> 00:01:52.862
That, you know, it's the Barbie movie

00:01:52.862 --> 00:01:54.447
that no one asked for,

00:01:54.447 --> 00:01:56.574
that no one thought we needed,

00:01:56.574 --> 00:01:57.992
you know, And.

00:01:57.992 --> 00:02:00.620
Kind of subversively seriously feminist.

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:02.789
Yes. Yes. And nobody believed that.

00:02:02.789 --> 00:02:03.706
They thought it was just going to be

00:02:03.706 --> 00:02:04.833
another telling of a

00:02:04.833 --> 00:02:06.251
of a really incredible doll

00:02:06.251 --> 00:02:07.961
that so many millions of girls

00:02:07.961 --> 00:02:09.379
around the world played with. Yeah.

00:02:09.379 --> 00:02:11.047
And it could have been that,

00:02:11.047 --> 00:02:11.506
you know, it

00:02:11.506 --> 00:02:13.091
very easily could have been something

00:02:13.091 --> 00:02:14.968
bright and fun and exciting

00:02:14.968 --> 00:02:16.136
and probably would have

00:02:16.136 --> 00:02:18.721
made a lot of money and been successful.

00:02:18.721 --> 00:02:19.180
But.

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:21.349
But what Gretta and her partner

00:02:21.349 --> 00:02:22.934
Noah, did with the script

00:02:22.934 --> 00:02:24.644
and then Greg as the director

00:02:24.644 --> 00:02:26.354
creating this world.

00:02:26.354 --> 00:02:27.939
It was so generous

00:02:27.939 --> 00:02:29.566
and it was so exciting.

00:02:29.566 --> 00:02:32.360
And, you know, as an adult woman, mother,

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:33.611
you know, to get

00:02:33.611 --> 00:02:35.238
a third of the way into the script

00:02:35.238 --> 00:02:37.073
and then to meet this

00:02:37.073 --> 00:02:41.035
this adult real flawed,

00:02:41.119 --> 00:02:44.622
you know, insecure, but having ambition.

00:02:44.622 --> 00:02:47.292
Woman like struggling to be,

00:02:47.292 --> 00:02:48.501
you know, so many things

00:02:48.501 --> 00:02:50.503
to see so many different people.

00:02:50.503 --> 00:02:53.840
It was so exciting to feel like

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:55.967
we had a voice in the story

00:02:55.967 --> 00:02:57.552
and you know, that

00:02:57.552 --> 00:02:58.845
I felt that way

00:02:58.845 --> 00:02:59.804
independent of being

00:02:59.804 --> 00:03:00.680
asked to be a part of it

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:02.140
just as a woman in the world.

00:03:02.140 --> 00:03:04.517
So we played a little bit of that speech.

00:03:04.517 --> 00:03:06.394
What would you say

00:03:06.394 --> 00:03:07.687
is the standout line,

00:03:07.687 --> 00:03:10.273
the stand out message that you wanted

00:03:10.273 --> 00:03:11.858
and maybe now really want

00:03:11.858 --> 00:03:12.984
even more to get across to

00:03:12.984 --> 00:03:15.111
young girls and even men?

00:03:15.195 --> 00:03:16.112
Yeah, I mean,

00:03:16.112 --> 00:03:16.571
that's the

00:03:16.571 --> 00:03:18.907
other piece of the generosity of this

00:03:18.907 --> 00:03:21.910
is that Greta and no one did It

00:03:21.910 --> 00:03:23.036
didn't only make

00:03:23.036 --> 00:03:24.329
Cam the butt of the joke.

00:03:24.329 --> 00:03:26.039
Everyone's the butt of a joke.

00:03:26.039 --> 00:03:27.123
At some point,

00:03:27.123 --> 00:03:28.750
they gave him a real reason

00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:29.667
to be in the story.

00:03:29.667 --> 00:03:30.543
They they

00:03:30.543 --> 00:03:32.962
they created a foil to the

00:03:32.962 --> 00:03:34.297
to the women's journey.

00:03:34.297 --> 00:03:36.633
To the journey about femininity

00:03:36.633 --> 00:03:40.011
and had this whole beautiful journey

00:03:40.011 --> 00:03:41.179
about masculinity

00:03:41.179 --> 00:03:42.972
and what it means for this character

00:03:42.972 --> 00:03:43.848
that Ryan Gosling

00:03:43.848 --> 00:03:45.308
obviously played

00:03:45.308 --> 00:03:48.102
with, like Marlon Brando commitment.

00:03:48.102 --> 00:03:49.979
It was so a mix. Yes.

00:03:49.979 --> 00:03:51.856
And so amazing to watch.

00:03:51.856 --> 00:03:54.359
I do feel like the message

00:03:54.359 --> 00:03:55.777
is deeply universal.

00:03:55.777 --> 00:03:56.986
It's not just for women

00:03:56.986 --> 00:03:58.071
or just for girls.

00:03:58.071 --> 00:04:00.281
It's like we all lose

00:04:00.281 --> 00:04:03.534
when we live inside of these boxes.

00:04:03.534 --> 00:04:05.745
Literal and imagined,

00:04:05.745 --> 00:04:07.997
and that we all are yearning

00:04:07.997 --> 00:04:11.000
and wanting to be more than the boxes

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:12.043
that we get stuck in

00:04:12.043 --> 00:04:13.586
and we keep ourselves in.

00:04:13.586 --> 00:04:15.255
There's some controversy.

00:04:15.255 --> 00:04:16.005
Judd Apatow,

00:04:16.005 --> 00:04:17.131
the wonderful,

00:04:17.131 --> 00:04:20.134
you know, film and comedic personality,

00:04:20.176 --> 00:04:21.803
has basically said,

00:04:21.803 --> 00:04:24.055
you know, the idea of of the academy,

00:04:24.055 --> 00:04:24.973
the academy Awards,

00:04:24.973 --> 00:04:26.349
putting Barbie in the adapted

00:04:26.349 --> 00:04:28.434
screenplay category for the Oscars

00:04:28.434 --> 00:04:30.311
instead of original screenplay,

00:04:30.311 --> 00:04:31.980
he said on Twitter X

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:33.940
that it's insulting to writers.

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:35.275
Do you agree?

00:04:35.275 --> 00:04:36.776
You know, I'm not a writer

00:04:36.776 --> 00:04:38.111
and I don't know the rules

00:04:38.111 --> 00:04:40.446
and the ins and outs of the academy.

00:04:40.446 --> 00:04:41.781
I think that it

00:04:41.781 --> 00:04:44.409
you know, that there was no story before.

00:04:44.409 --> 00:04:44.784
And Greta

00:04:44.784 --> 00:04:45.952
got her hands on this

00:04:45.952 --> 00:04:46.369
to think

00:04:46.369 --> 00:04:48.955
that anyone handed anything to Greta,

00:04:48.955 --> 00:04:49.998
if anything,

00:04:49.998 --> 00:04:52.458
this IP and the fact that it was Barbie

00:04:52.458 --> 00:04:53.835
was a bigger obstacle

00:04:53.835 --> 00:04:56.879
towards creating an exciting new kind

00:04:56.879 --> 00:04:59.173
of groundbreaking story and character.

00:04:59.173 --> 00:05:02.844
It's wholly and completely original,

00:05:02.885 --> 00:05:04.804
and I think that it should have been

00:05:04.804 --> 00:05:06.848
in the category that the writers chose

00:05:06.848 --> 00:05:08.391
to submit it in.

00:05:08.391 --> 00:05:09.183
That said, I'm

00:05:09.183 --> 00:05:11.519
not an Academy rules expert and I.

00:05:11.519 --> 00:05:12.395
I don't know.

00:05:12.395 --> 00:05:12.895
You.

00:05:12.895 --> 00:05:14.897
You, I told Mark Ronson,

00:05:14.897 --> 00:05:16.149
the music producer, there's

00:05:16.149 --> 00:05:18.651
going to be a before baby and after I.

00:05:18.651 --> 00:05:19.569
Did tell him.

00:05:19.569 --> 00:05:21.070
So how did the world change before

00:05:21.070 --> 00:05:22.822
and after and how did America Ferrera,

00:05:22.822 --> 00:05:23.740
his World change?

00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:25.074
Oh, wow.

00:05:25.074 --> 00:05:26.951
Those are two very big questions.

00:05:26.951 --> 00:05:30.913
I mean, I think that

00:05:30.997 --> 00:05:32.582
only time will tell

00:05:32.582 --> 00:05:33.082
what the

00:05:33.082 --> 00:05:36.210
real cultural impact and individual

00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:40.006
impact for for people watching this film

00:05:40.089 --> 00:05:40.631
can have.

00:05:40.631 --> 00:05:43.176
I know that as an artist,

00:05:43.176 --> 00:05:46.179
it is so deeply inspiring.

00:05:46.262 --> 00:05:49.515
It's creativity, it's cinematic artistry,

00:05:49.599 --> 00:05:52.310
and also just the rules that it breaks,

00:05:52.310 --> 00:05:52.727
you know?

00:05:52.727 --> 00:05:54.604
And and I do think that it was

00:05:54.604 --> 00:05:56.314
it was that bridesmaid's moment,

00:05:56.314 --> 00:05:56.647
you know,

00:05:56.647 --> 00:05:57.315
where where

00:05:57.315 --> 00:05:58.066
before that

00:05:58.066 --> 00:05:59.609
